      "question_category": "edge_cases_complexity",
      "stem": "For the naive recursive Fibonacci function (`int fib(int n) { if (n <= 1) return n; return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2); }`), what are its time complexity and auxiliary space complexity (maximum simultaneous call-stack depth)?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Time complexity is `Theta(2^n)` and space complexity is `O(1)`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Both the time complexity and the auxiliary call-stack space complexity of naive `fib(n)` are `Theta(2^n)` exponential because all `Theta(phi^n)` frames coexist in memory."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Time complexity is `Theta(n^2)` and space complexity is `Theta(n)`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Time complexity is `Theta(phi^n)`, whereas auxiliary space complexity is `Theta(n)` since only one root-to-leaf call-stack branch is active at any instant."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "A very common student misconception is thinking that `O(2^n)` total recursive calls require `O(2^n)` stack space. In C++, `fib(n - 1)` must completely finish and pop all its stack frames before `fib(n - 2)` is ever called. Therefore, the maximum number of simultaneous frames on the call stack equals the height of the recursion tree, which is `n + 1 = Theta(n)`.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Each active frame along the deepest path `fib(n) -> fib(n-1) -> ... -> fib(1)` consumes stack memory (`Theta(n)`).",
        "B": "Confuses the total number of nodes in the recursion tree (`~phi^n`) with the maximum height of the tree (`n`) active on the call stack at one time.",
        "C": "Total calls grow exponentially `~1.618^n`, not quadratically."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
