      "question_category": "tracing_application",
      "stem": "Consider converting the non-tail-recursive array sum `int sumRec(const int* A, int n)` into a tail-recursive helper `int sumTail(const int* A, int n, int acc)` by introducing an accumulator parameter `acc`. Which implementation is correct when called as `sumTail(A, n, 0)`?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "`if (n == 0) return acc; return sumTail(A, n, acc + A[n - 1]);`"
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "`if (n == 0) return 0; return sumTail(A, n - 1, acc + A[n - 1]);`"
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "`if (n == 0) return acc; return A[n - 1] + sumTail(A, n - 1, acc);`"
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "`if (n == 0) return acc; return sumTail(A, n - 1, acc + A[n - 1]);`"
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "In the accumulator pattern, each step folds `A[n - 1]` into `acc` (`acc + A[n - 1]`) before recursing on `n - 1`. When the base case `n == 0` is reached, `acc` holds the complete sum and must be returned (`return acc;`).",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Fails to decrement `n`, causing infinite recursion.",
        "B": "Returning `0` at the base case `n == 0` throws away the entire accumulated sum in `acc` and always returns `0`.",
        "C": "Leaves `A[n - 1] + ...` outside the call (still non-tail-recursive) and never updates `acc`."
      },
