      "learning_objective_id": "T08_2_LO2",
      "question_category": "tracing_application",
      "stem": "Consider the recursive function:\n```cpp\nvoid buildList(Node*& head, int n) {\n    if (n <= 0) return;\n    head = new Node{n, head};\n    buildList(head, n - 1);\n}\n```\nIs `buildList` tail-recursive, and what list does `buildList(head, 4)` construct when `head` is initially `nullptr`?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Yes, `buildList(head, n - 1)` is a tail call; it pushes `4, 3, 2, 1` at the front, yielding `head -> [1] -> [2] -> [3] -> [4] -> nullptr`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "No, `buildList(head, n - 1)` is not tail-recursive primarily because it allocates heap memory with `new Node` before invoking the recursive call."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Yes, `buildList(head, n - 1)` is a tail call, and it produces the descending list `head -> [4] -> [3] -> [2] -> [1] -> nullptr`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "It leaks nodes 4, 3, and 2."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "A",
      "answer_explanation": "Because `head = new Node{n, head};` executes before `buildList(head, n - 1);` and nothing happens after `buildList` returns, it is strictly tail-recursive (equivalent to `for (; n > 0; --n) head = new Node{n, head};`). Pushing `4`, then `3`, then `2`, then `1` onto the front of `head` leaves `1` at the front (`[1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4]`).",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "B": "Doing work *before* the recursive call does not prevent a call from being in tail position.",
        "C": "`4` is pushed first (becoming the tail), and `1` is pushed last (becoming the new `head`).",
        "D": "Each new node's `next` pointer is set to the previous `head`, linking all 4 nodes."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "tail_recursive_list_construction_trace"
      ],
