      "concept_id": "C09",
      "topic_id": "T09_1",
      "learning_objective_id": "T09_1_LO2",
      "question_category": "tracing_application",
      "stem": "When merging two already-sorted subarrays `L` of length `m` and `R` of length `m` (total `2m` distinct elements) using the standard two-pointer `merge` algorithm, what are the minimum (best-case) and maximum (worst-case) numbers of element comparisons `L[i] <= R[j]` performed before one subarray is exhausted and the remainder of the other is copied directly?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Minimum: `1` comparison; Maximum: `m^2` comparisons."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Minimum: `m` comparisons; Maximum: `2m - 1` comparisons."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Minimum: `2m` comparisons; Maximum: `2m` comparisons."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Minimum: `m - 1` comparisons; Maximum: `m + 1` comparisons."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "B",
      "answer_explanation": "Each comparison `L[i] <= R[j]` advances either `i` or `j` by 1 and places one element into the output. The loop stops as soon as either `i == m` or `j == m`. The fastest exhaustion takes `m` comparisons (one side advances `m` times in a row); the slowest exhaustion leaves 1 element in one array when the other reaches `m`, taking `(m - 1) + m = 2m - 1` comparisons. Notice both `m` and `2m - 1` are `Theta(m)`.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Exhausting an array of size `m` requires advancing a pointer `m` times (`>= m` comparisons), and never exceeds `2m - 1`.",
        "C": "Once one subarray is exhausted, the remaining elements of the other subarray are copied without any further key comparisons (`2m` comparisons never occurs).",
        "D": "For `m = 3`, `[1, 3, 5]` and `[2, 4, 6]` compares `(1,2), (3,2), (3,4), (5,4), (5,6)` = `5 = 2(3) - 1` comparisons."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
