      "question_category": "tracing_application",
      "stem": "In Quick Sort, if the pivot is always chosen as the first element (`A[low]`) or the last element (`A[high]`), what input distribution triggers the worst-case `Theta(n^2)` running time, and what recurrence describes it?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "First/last pivot Quick Sort never takes `Theta(n^2)` time."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "A uniformly random permutation of `n` distinct integers, where every partition produces a `0` and `n - 1` split in `Theta(n^2)` time."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "An array where the median is always at `A[low]`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "An already sorted array, where every partition produces a `0` and `n - 1` split (`T(n) = T(n - 1) + Theta(n) = Theta(n^2)`)."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "When `A` is already sorted (or reverse-sorted), picking `A[low]` or `A[high]` selects the extreme minimum or maximum of the subarray as the pivot every single time. Partitioning does `Theta(n)` work to reduce the problem size by only `1` (`n -> n - 1 -> n - 2 -> ...`), summing to `n + (n-1) + ... + 1 = Theta(n^2)`.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Unbalanced `0 : n-1` splits degrade to `Theta(n^2)`.",
        "B": "A random permutation gives `Theta(n log n)` expected time.",
        "C": "If the median is always at the pivot position, every split is `n/2 : n/2`, achieving the best case `Theta(n log n)`."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "easy",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "quicksort_sorted_input_worst_case_quadratic"
      ],
