      "learning_objective_id": "T09_2_LO2",
      "question_category": "tracing_application",
      "stem": "In 3-way Dutch National Flag partitioning (`lt = low, i = low, gt = high`) around `pivot`, the loop maintains:\n- `A[low .. lt - 1] < pivot`\n- `A[lt .. i - 1] == pivot`\n- `A[i .. gt]` is unclassified\n- `A[gt + 1 .. high] > pivot`\nWhen `A[i] > pivot` and we execute `std::swap(A[i], A[gt--]);`, why do we NOT increment `++i` in that branch (unlike `A[i] < pivot` which executes `std::swap(A[lt++], A[i++]);`)?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Since the element swapped into `A[i]` from `A[gt]` came from the unclassified region and has not yet been compared against `pivot`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Because incrementing `++i` after swapping with `A[gt]` would immediately cause an out-of-bounds array access at index `high + 1`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "We actually DO increment `++i` in all three branches (`<`, `==`, `>`)."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Because `gt` is incremented instead of decremented."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "A",
      "answer_explanation": "When `A[i] < pivot`, `A[lt]` is known to equal `pivot` (for `lt < i`), so after `swap(A[lt], A[i])`, the new `A[i]` is `== pivot` and both `lt++` and `i++` can advance. In contrast, when `A[i] > pivot`, `A[gt]` has not been inspected yet. After `swap(A[i], A[gt])` and `gt--`, the next iteration must stay at `i` to inspect the newly brought-in `A[i]`.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "B": "The loop condition is `while (i <= gt)`.",
        "C": "Incrementing `++i` after `swap(A[i], A[gt--])` skips inspecting the old `A[gt]`, leaving `< pivot` or `> pivot` elements in the middle `== pivot` region.",
        "D": "`gt` is decremented (`gt--`) to expand the `> pivot` suffix."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
