      "question_category": "concept_invariant",
      "stem": "In Phase 1 of Heap Sort (Floyd's Bottom-Up `Build-Max-Heap`), which range of 0-based indices calls `siftDown(A, n, i)`, in what direction, and what is the tight asymptotic time complexity of building the heap?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "`for (int i = 0; i < n / 2; ++i) siftDown(A, n, i);` (from `0` up to `n/2 - 1`), running in `Theta(n)` time."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "`for (int i = n / 2 - 1; i >= 0; --i) siftDown(A, n, i);`, running in `Theta(n)` linear time."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "`for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; --i) siftUp(A, i);`, running in `Theta(n)` time."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Bottom-Up `Build-Max-Heap` always takes `Theta(n log n)` time because `n / 2` nodes each sift down `log n` levels."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "B",
      "answer_explanation": "Leaves (`indices floor(n/2) .. n-1`) are already trivial 1-node heaps of height 0. Calling `siftDown` backward from `i = floor(n/2) - 1` down to `0` guarantees that both child subtrees of `i` are already valid Max-Heaps when `siftDown(i)` runs. Because `ceil(n / 2^(h+1))` nodes have height `h`, total work is bounded by `sum_{h=0}^{log n} (n / 2^(h+1)) * O(h) = O(n) * sum (h / 2^h) = Theta(n)`.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Running `siftDown` top-down from `i = 0` to `n/2 - 1` fails because `siftDown(i)` requires `left(i)` and `right(i)` to already be valid heaps.",
        "C": "`siftUp` from top to bottom (`i = 0 .. n-1`) takes `Theta(n log n)` worst-case time because the `n/2` leaves at the bottom each travel `log2 n` levels up to the root.",
        "D": "`O(n log n)` is a loose upper bound; because most nodes are near the bottom (`height 0, 1, 2`), the exact tight bound for Floyd's bottom-up construction is `Theta(n)` (at most `2n` comparisons)."
      },
