      "learning_objective_id": "T09_4_LO1",
      "question_category": "concept_invariant",
      "stem": "In Least-Significant-Digit (LSD) Radix Sort, which processes `d` digits from the rightmost (least significant) digit to the leftmost (most significant) digit, what property MUST the single-digit subroutine sort (such as Counting Sort / Bucket Sort) satisfy for LSD Radix Sort to be correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "The single-digit sort MUST be stable, so when two keys share the same digit in pass `p`, their relative order from lower-order digits `1.. p-1` is kept."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "The single-digit subroutine sort must be an in-place unstable swap-based Selection Sort so that auxiliary memory remains `O(1)` across all `d` passes."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "The single-digit subroutine sort must process digits from most significant to least significant without using any bucket arrays."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Stability is optional; any unstable `O(n)` digit sort produces a correctly sorted array."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "A",
      "answer_explanation": "Proof of correctness by induction on pass `p`: after pass `p - 1`, numbers are sorted by their lowest `p - 1` digits. On pass `p`, numbers with *different* `p`-th digits are placed in strictly increasing order of their `p`-th digit, while numbers with the *same* `p`-th digit must remain in ascending order of their lowest `p - 1` digits—which holds if and only if pass `p` is stable.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "B": "Swap-based Selection Sort is unstable and `O(n^2)`, which would both scramble lower digits and ruin linear time.",
        "C": "LSD Radix Sort specifically processes from least significant to most significant digit.",
        "D": "If pass 2 (tens digit) is unstable, `[12, 19]` (already sorted by units digit `2 < 9` in pass 1) can be reordered into `[19, 12]` because both have tens digit `1`."
      },
