      "learning_objective_id": "T09_4_LO2",
      "question_category": "tracing_application",
      "stem": "In the Counting Sort subroutine used inside Radix Sort, after computing the cumulative prefix-sum frequency array `cnt[0 .. k-1]` (where `cnt[d]` stores the number of elements with digit `<= d`), the placement loop writes:\n```cpp\nfor (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; --i) {\n    int d = digit(A[i]);\n    out[--cnt[d]] = A[i];\n}\n```\nWhy does this loop iterate backward from `i = n - 1` down to `0` rather than forward from `i = 0` to `n - 1`?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Iterating backward reduces the time complexity from `Theta(n^2)` to `Theta(n)`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Scanning `A` forward from `0` to `n - 1` while decrementing `--cnt[d]` places the earliest occurrence of digit `d` into the rightmost slot of bucket `d`, which still preserves stability."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Since `--cnt[d]` fills bucket `d` from right to left, scanning `A` backward from `n - 1` down to `0` places the latest occurrence of digit `d` into the rightmost slot, preserving stability."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Iteration direction does not affect stability; scanning forward `for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) out[--cnt[d]] = A[i];` preserves the exact same relative order of equal keys."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "answer_explanation": "Since `cnt[d]` initially points to the end of the range for digit `d` and decrements (`--cnt[d]`) on each write, the slots for digit `d` are filled right-to-left. To keep equal-digit elements in their original left-to-right order, we must visit the rightmost occurrence in `A` first (`i = n - 1` down to `0`). If you iterate forward `i = 0 .. n-1` while decrementing `--cnt[d]`, every group of equal digits is placed in reverse order (unstable, breaking LSD Radix Sort.).",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Both directions run in `Theta(n)` time.",
        "B": "Forward iteration stays within bounds `0 .. n-1`, making the instability bug silent and dangerous.",
        "D": "Iterating forward with `--cnt[d]` is anti-stable (it reverses the relative order of equal keys on every pass.)."
      },
