      "learning_objective_id": "T10_2_LO1",
      "question_category": "concept_invariant",
      "stem": "A student writes the following recursive function to test whether a binary tree (with distinct keys) is a valid Binary Search Tree (BST):\n```cpp\nbool isBSTLocalBug(Node* r) {\n    if (!r) return true;\n    if (r->left && r->left->key >= r->key) return false;\n    if (r->right && r->right->key <= r->key) return false;\n    return isBSTLocalBug(r->left) && isBSTLocalBug(r->right);\n}\n```\nWhy is `isBSTLocalBug` incorrect, and which 4-node tree is a counterexample where it falsely returns `true`?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "It checks only local parent-child inequalities instead of the global ancestor range `(minVal, maxVal)`; e.g., Root `20` with left child `10` having right child `25` satisfies `10 < 20` and `25 > 10` locally, but `25` violates `25 < 20`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "It falsely returns `false` on valid Binary Search Trees whenever a left child has a non-null right grandchild."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "A BST requires `r->left->key > r->key`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "The function is actually 100% correct for all binary trees."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "A",
      "answer_explanation": "The BST invariant is a global subtree property: every node in `r->left` (not just the immediate left child.) must have `key < r->key`, and every node in `r->right` must have `key > r->key`. In `20 -> left: 10 -> right: 25`, `25` is locally greater than its parent `10`, but globally greater than its ancestor `20`. Correct validation passes down `(lo, hi)` bounds (`isValidBST(r->left, lo, r->key)` and `isValidBST(r->right, r->key, hi)`) or checks that Inorder traversal is strictly increasing.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "B": "Every valid BST satisfies the local check; the bug is false positives on invalid trees.",
        "C": "BSTs place smaller keys on the left.",
        "D": "Failing to propagate ancestor bounds `(lo, hi)` is the most famous BST validation bug."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "bst_local_vs_global_invariant_trap"
      ],
      "authorship_type": "AI-assisted team-authored",
      "reference_sources": [
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binary_search_tree",
        "https://visualgo.net/"
      ],
      "verification_method": "code_execution_trace",
