      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "answer_explanation": "Whenever a node underflows (0 keys), we first check if an immediate left or right sibling has a spare key (`>= 2` keys). If so, a key rotation through the parent (`s -> parent -> u`) fixes the underflow locally in `O(1)` time without changing the number of children of `parent`. Only if adjacent siblings have just 1 key (`minimum occupancy`) do we merge `u + parent_separator + s` into a single node (which removes a key from `parent`).",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "`u` already has `0` keys (underflow), so it has nothing to promote.",
        "B": "Leaving a parent with `k` keys and only `k` children (or deleting a leaf at depth `h`) violates the multiway search tree invariants.",
        "D": "Key transfer between siblings in a search tree must pass through the parent separator key (`s -> parent -> u`) to preserve `left < parent < right` ordering."
      },
