      "concept_id": "C10",
      "topic_id": "T10_5",
      "learning_objective_id": "T10_5_LO2",
      "question_category": "tracing_application",
      "stem": "Inside a single B-Tree node stored in RAM that currently contains `k` sorted keys (`k` up to `2t - 1`, e.g., `k = 500`), how should the algorithm locate which child pointer `c_i` to follow, and what are the CPU time and Disk I/O costs of one node step?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Run Quick Sort on the node's keys on every search."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Issue `k` separate Disk I/O read system calls to fetch each of the `k` keys inside the node one by one during a linear scan, taking `O(t * log_t N)` Disk I/Os."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Run Binary Search on the node's in-RAM sorted array `keys[0.. k-1]`, taking `O(log t)` CPU comparisons and `1` Disk I/O per level."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Use hardware hashing inside each B-tree node so that total CPU comparisons across the entire root-to-leaf search is `O(1)` independent of `N`."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "answer_explanation": "Once the single disk I/O loads the entire B-tree node into RAM (`1` disk I/O per level), its `k <= 2t - 1` keys are already in a sorted contiguous array in memory. Binary search inside the node takes `O(log_2 t)` CPU time. Multiplying by `log_t N` levels gives `log_t(N) * log_2(t) = log_2(N)` total CPU comparisons—the exact same asymptotic CPU comparison count as a binary search tree, while slashing disk I/Os by a factor of `log_2(t)`.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Keys inside a B-tree node are maintained in sorted order at all times.",
        "B": "All `k` keys reside inside the same single disk block/page, loaded in 1 I/O transfer.",
        "D": "Searching `N` keys by comparison still requires `Omega(log_2 N)` CPU comparisons."
      },
