      "question_category": "tradeoff_choice",
      "stem": "In asymptotic graph algorithm analysis, a family of graphs with `|V| = V` and `|E| = E` is informally classified as sparse vs dense based on how `E` scales with `V`. Which statement accurately captures this distinction?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Sparse graphs only occur in directed graphs; undirected graphs are always dense."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "A graph is sparse if and only if it is disconnected (`E < V - 1`)."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "A graph is dense whenever `E >= V`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "A graph is sparse when `E = O(V)`, and dense when `E = Theta(V^2)`."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "Because `0 <= E <= O(V^2)` in simple graphs, algorithms with `O((V + E) log V)` vs `O(V^2)` runtime behave differently depending on whether `E` grows linearly with `V` (sparse, `E = O(V)`) or quadratically (dense, `E = Theta(V^2)`).",
