      "learning_objective_id": "T12_1_LO1",
      "question_category": "concept_invariant",
      "stem": "Which auxiliary data structure is used by Breadth-First Search (BFS) to manage the frontier of discovered vertices, and in what order are vertices explored relative to their unweighted distance (number of edges) from the source vertex `s`?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "A FIFO Queue, which guarantees that BFS explores vertices in non-decreasing order of distance `d[s, v]`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "A LIFO Stack (`std::stack`), which explores the deepest unvisited path first."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "A Min-Heap Priority Queue (`std::priority_queue`), which is required even when all edges have unweighted cost `1`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "A Hash Table, which explores vertices in random hash bucket order."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "A",
      "answer_explanation": "BFS uses a FIFO Queue (`std::queue`). By induction on the queue invariant, the queue at any moment holds vertices of at most two adjacent distance values: if the head vertex has distance `d`, every other vertex in the queue has distance either `d` or `d + 1`. This monotonic property guarantees that vertices are discovered and finalized in strictly non-decreasing order of shortest-path distance.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "B": "Using a LIFO stack yields Depth-First Search (DFS), which does NOT explore vertices in distance order.",
        "C": "When all edges have unit weight `1`, a plain FIFO queue automatically maintains sorted distance order in `O(1)` per push/pop without `O(log V)` heap overhead.",
        "D": "Hash tables do not preserve FIFO wavefront order."
      },
