      "learning_objective_id": "T12_1_LO2",
      "question_category": "code_trace",
      "stem": "Consider two ways of marking vertices as `visited` in C++ BFS:\n- Method 1 (Correct): Mark `visited[v] = true` immediately when `v` is discovered and pushed into the queue (`Q.push(v)`).\n- Method 2 (Flawed): Mark `visited[u] = true` only when `u` is popped from the front of the queue (`u = Q.front(); Q.pop();`).\nWhat bug occurs in Method 2 on a graph with cycles (such as a diamond `0-1, 0-2, 1-3, 2-3` or a dense graph)?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "There is no difference; Method 1 and Method 2 always push the exact same number of elements."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "In Method 2, marking `visited` only upon dequeue causes BFS to skip reachable vertices and terminate before visiting the entire component."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Method 2 turns BFS into Depth-First Search (DFS)."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "In Method 2, while `v` waits in the queue, other vertices at the same level see `visited[v] == false` and push duplicate copies of `v`, blowing up queue size to `O(E)` and corrupting `parent`/`dist`."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "In the diamond graph `0 -> {1, 2} -> 3`, when `1` is popped, `3` is pushed into `Q` (`Q = [2, 3]`). If `visited[3]` is not set until `3` is popped, then when `2` is popped next, `visited[3]` is still `false`, so `3` is pushed a second time (`Q = [3, 3]`). Marking `visited[v] = true` at enqueue time guarantees each vertex is pushed at most once.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "On a complete graph `K_V`, Method 2 pushes `Theta(V^2)` items into the queue instead of `V` items.",
        "B": "Method 2 still visits all reachable vertices, it just visits and pushes them multiple times.",
        "C": "It still uses a FIFO queue, not a stack."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
