      "question_category": "concept_invariant",
      "stem": "Suppose we run BFS from source `s` on an undirected graph `G = (V, E)`. If `(u, v) in E` is any edge whose endpoints `u` and `v` are both reachable from `s`, what are the only possible values for the difference in their BFS shortest-path distances `|d[u] - d[v]|`?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "`|d[u] - d[v]|` can be any integer between `0` and `|V| - 1`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "`|d[u] - d[v]|` must always equal `1`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "`|d[u] - d[v]|` can only be `0` or `1`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "`|d[u] - d[v]|` can be `0, 1, or 2`."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "answer_explanation": "Suppose without loss of generality that `d[u] <= d[v]`. Since `{u, v}` is an edge of weight 1, the triangle inequality gives `d[v] <= d[u] + 1`. Because `d[u]` and `d[v]` are integers with `d[u] <= d[v] <= d[u] + 1`, the difference `d[v] - d[u]` is either `0` (same-level cross edge, e.g., in an odd cycle like `K_3`) or `1` (adjacent-level tree or cross edge).",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "If `d[v] >= d[u] + 2`, then when `u` was dequeued at distance `d[u]`, its neighbor `v` would have been discovered at distance `d[u] + 1 < d[v]`, a contradiction.",
        "B": "In a triangle `s - u - v - s`, both `u` and `v` have `d[u] = d[v] = 1`, so `|d[u] - d[v]| = 0` for edge `{u, v}`.",
        "D": "A difference of `2` is impossible by the same triangle-inequality argument."
      },
