      "question_id": "Q_T12_1_10",
      "question_family_id": "QF_T12_1_F09",
      "concept_id": "C12",
      "topic_id": "T12_1",
      "learning_objective_id": "T12_1_LO1",
      "question_category": "code_trace",
      "stem": "Why does a standard FIFO-queue BFS fail to guarantee shortest paths when edges have non-uniform positive weights (for example, triangle `0 -> 1` with weight `10`, `0 -> 2` with weight `2`, and `2 -> 1` with weight `3`), and why does it work on unweighted graphs?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "BFS fails because `0 -> 2 -> 1` is a cycle."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "BFS fails on weighted graphs primarily because `std::queue` is never able to store `(vertex, weight)` pairs and enters an infinite loop whenever edge weights differ from `1`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "BFS only fails when edge weights are negative; on any graph with positive edge weights `{10, 2, 3}`, standard FIFO BFS finds the minimum-weight path."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "BFS minimizes hop count, not edge weight: from `0`, BFS discovers `1` in 1 hop (`w = 10`) and marks `1` visited before exploring `0 -> 2 -> 1` of weight `2 + 3 = 5`."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "Standard BFS equates path length with edge count (`1` per edge). On `0 -> 1 (10)`, `0 -> 2 (2)`, `2 -> 1 (3)`, `1` is discovered directly from `0` at hop distance `1` (weight `10`), whereas the true shortest weighted path `0 -> 2 -> 1` has 2 hops and total weight `5`. Non-uniform non-negative weights require Dijkstra's algorithm (`std::priority_queue`).",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "`0 -> 2 -> 1` is a DAG (no directed cycle).",
        "B": "`std::queue` can hold any C++ type, including `std::pair<int, int>`; the issue is FIFO ordering vs min-distance ordering.",
        "C": "As shown by the counterexample `w(0,1)=10 > w(0,2)+w(2,1)=2+3=5`, BFS fails as soon as a 2-edge path is lighter than a 1-edge path."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "hard",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "MC_BFS_HOPS_VS_WEIGHTED_SHORTEST_PATH"
      ],
      "authorship_type": "AI-assisted team-authored",
      "reference_sources": [
        "https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Catalog/html/index.html",
        "https://cp-algorithms.com/graph/breadth-first-search.html"
      ],
      "verification_method": "compile_and_run",
      "agent_check_status": "passed",
      "human_review_status": "pending",
      "approved_by": null,
