      "question_category": "concept_invariant",
      "stem": "During a 3-color DFS (`WHITE` = undiscovered, `GRAY` = discovered and currently active on the recursion call stack, `BLACK` = finished) on a directed graph, how is a newly explored directed edge `(u, v)` (where `u` is currently `GRAY`) classified based on the color of `v` at the moment `(u, v)` is inspected?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Directed graphs only have Tree edges and Back edges in DFS."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "If `v` is `BLACK`, `(u, v)` is always a Back edge to an ancestor; if `v` is `GRAY`, `(u, v)` is always a Cross edge between unrelated subtrees."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "If `v` is `GRAY`, `(u, v)` is a Forward edge."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "If `v` is `WHITE`, `(u, v)` is a Tree edge; if `v` is `GRAY`, a Back edge; if `v` is `BLACK`, a Forward or Cross edge."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "When exploring `(u, v)` from active vertex `u` (`GRAY`): (1) `v == WHITE` means `v` is newly discovered via tree edge `(u, v)`; (2) `v == GRAY` means `v` is currently on the active recursion stack (an ancestor of `u`), so `(u, v)` is a back edge; (3) `v == BLACK` means `v` has already finished: if `d[u] < d[v]`, `v` was discovered after `u` entered (so `v` is a proper descendant of `u`: forward edge); if `d[u] > d[v]`, `v` finished before `u` was related to it (cross edge).",
