      "answer_explanation": "If `(u, v)` is a back edge, `v` is an active `GRAY` ancestor of `u` in the DFS tree, so the tree path `v ~> u` plus `(u, v)` forms a directed cycle. Conversely, if `G` has a directed cycle `C`, let `v` be the first vertex of `C` discovered by DFS (`d[v]` minimum on `C`). By the White-Path Theorem, the predecessor `u` of `v` on `C` becomes a descendant of `v` before `v` turns `BLACK`, so `(u, v)` is explored while `v` is still `GRAY`—producing a back edge.",
