      "learning_objective_id": "T12_2_LO1",
      "question_category": "code_trace",
      "stem": "Suppose we want to convert recursive `DFS(0)` into an iterative DFS using an explicit `std::stack<int> S` so that it visits vertices in the exact same discovery order as recursive DFS (which explores `adj[u]` from index `0` to `adj[u].size() - 1`). If we push all unvisited neighbors of `u` onto `S` when `u` is popped, in what order must we push `adj[u]` onto the LIFO stack `S`?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Iterate through `adj[u]` in reverse order when pushing onto stack `S`, so `adj[u][0]` ends up on top of the LIFO stack and is popped first."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Iterate through `adj[u]` in forward order (`0` to `adj[u].size() - 1`) when pushing onto stack `S`, because `std::stack` pops elements in FIFO order."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Sort `adj[u]` by vertex degree before pushing."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Use `std::queue<int>` instead of `std::stack<int>`."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "A",
      "answer_explanation": "Because a stack is Last-In First-Out (LIFO), if `adj[0] = [1, 2]` and we push `1` then `2`, `2` sits on top of the stack and gets visited *before* `1` (reverse DFS order). Pushing in reverse order (`push(2)` then `push(1)`) places `1` on top of the stack so `1`'s entire subtree is explored before `2`, matching recursive DFS.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "B": "A stack is LIFO, not FIFO; pushing `0 .. deg(u)-1` visits neighbors in reverse order.",
        "C": "Degree sorting changes the traversal order.",
        "D": "`std::queue` performs BFS, not DFS."
      },
