      "concept_id": "C12",
      "topic_id": "T12_2",
      "learning_objective_id": "T12_2_LO3",
      "question_category": "concept_invariant",
      "stem": "In an undirected connected graph `G = (V, E)`, a vertex `u` is an articulation point (cut vertex) if removing `u` disconnects `G`, and an edge `e` is a bridge if removing `e` disconnects `G`. In Tarjan's `O(V + E)` DFS algorithm, what information does `low[u]` track to identify bridges and articulation points?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "`low[u]` tracks the minimum `d[w]` reachable from `u`'s DFS subtree using tree edges plus at most one back edge; tree edge `(u, v)` is a bridge iff `low[v] > d[u]`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "`low[u]` tracks the minimum 0-indexed integer vertex label in `u`'s connected component; tree edge `(u, v)` is a bridge if and exclusively if `low[v] == 0`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "A tree edge `(u, v)` is a bridge iff `(u, v)` is a Back edge."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "`low[u]` equals the finish time `f[u]` minus `d[u]`."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "A",
      "answer_explanation": "In a DFS tree of an undirected graph (which has only tree and back edges), removing a tree edge `(u, v)` disconnects `v`'s subtree from the rest of the graph if and only if no vertex in `v`'s subtree has a back edge to `u` or a proper ancestor of `u`—which is equivalent to `low[v] > d[u]` (and for non-root articulation point `u`, `low[v] >= d[u]`).",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "B": "Using raw vertex IDs instead of DFS discovery times `d[w]` fails whenever vertex IDs are not numbered in DFS preorder.",
        "C": "A Back edge can *never* be a bridge because its endpoints are already connected by the DFS tree path.",
        "D": "`f[u] - d[u]` measures subtree size, not back-edge reachability to ancestors."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "hard",
