      "question_category": "concept_invariant",
      "stem": "By the Cut Property, suppose `A` is a subset of edges of some MST of `G = (V, E)`, and `(S, V \\ S)` is any cut of `G` that respects `A` (no edge in `A` crosses the cut). If `(u, v)` is a light edge crossing `(S, V \\ S)` (its weight is minimum among all edges crossing that cut), what holds?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Every edge crossing the cut `(S, V \\ S)` belongs to the MST, and the lightest edge on any simple cycle is under no circumstances able to belong to any MST."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Edge `(u, v)` is safe for `A`; and by the Cycle Property, the strictly heaviest edge `e_max` on any simple cycle cannot belong to any MST."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "The heaviest edge crossing `(S, V \\ S)` is safe for `A`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "The Cut Property holds only when all edge weights are positive."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "B",
      "answer_explanation": "Both Kruskal's and Prim's algorithms are direct specializations of the Cut Property (exchange argument: if an MST `T` containing `A` did not include the light crossing edge `(u, v)`, adding `(u, v)` to `T` creates a cycle containing another edge `(x, y)` crossing `(S, V \\ S)` with `w(u, v) <= w(x, y)`, so `T' = T - {(x, y)} U {(u, v)}` is also an MST.). This holds for arbitrary real edge weights (positive, zero, or negative.).",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Only a *minimum-weight* (light) edge crossing a cut that respects `A` is guaranteed safe.",
        "C": "Picking the heaviest edge maximizes weight rather than minimizing it.",
        "D": "The Cut Property and both Kruskal's and Prim's algorithms work identically even when edge weights are negative."
      },
