      "question_category": "concept_invariant",
      "stem": "Every Minimum Spanning Tree `T` of a connected undirected weighted graph `G = (V, E)` automatically satisfies the Minimax Path (Bottleneck Shortest Path) property. What does this property guarantee?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "For every pair `u, v in V`, the unique path between `u` and `v` in the MST `T` minimizes the sum of edge weights (`sum_{e in path} w(e)`) over all `u-v` paths in `G`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "For every pair `u, v in V`, the unique path between `u` and `v` in the MST `T` minimizes the maximum single-edge weight over all `u-v` paths in `G`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "The heaviest edge in `T` is heavier than the heaviest edge outside `T`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Every MST has maximum vertex degree at most `2`."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "B",
      "answer_explanation": "Suppose the unique `u-v` path `P_T` in MST `T` has heaviest edge `e^*` cutting `T` into `(S, V \\ S)` with `u in S, v in V \\ S`. Since `T` is an MST, `e^*` is a minimum-weight edge crossing `(S, V \\ S)`. Any `u-v` path `P` in `G` must cross `(S, V \\ S)` at some edge `e'`, which must satisfy `w(e') >= w(e^*)`. Thus `P_T` minimizes the bottleneck (`max` edge weight) between *every* pair of vertices simultaneously.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Minimizing the *sum* of edge weights between `u` and `v` is the shortest-path problem (Dijkstra), which an MST does NOT guarantee (`u-v` of weight `10` vs `u-w-v` of weights `6 + 6 = 12`: MST uses `6+6`, whose max edge is `6 < 10`, even though its sum `12 > 10`.).",
        "C": "In fact, the MST minimizes the maximum edge weight needed to connect the graph.",
        "D": "A star graph `K_{1, V-1}` is an MST with maximum degree `V - 1`."
      },
