      "learning_objective_id": "T12_5_LO2",
      "question_category": "concept_invariant",
      "stem": "A student encounters a graph `G = (V, E)` containing a negative edge of weight `-5`, adds `+5` to the weight of every edge in `E` so all weights are `>= 0`, and runs Dijkstra's Algorithm on the shifted weights `w'(e) = w(e) + 5`. Why does this trick FAIL to find true shortest paths in `G`?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Dijkstra's algorithm still fails even when all shifted weights `w'(e)` are non-negative."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Adding `+5` to every edge only works if we multiply edge weights by `5` instead."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Adding `+5` to every edge preserves all shortest paths in `G` for the exact same reason that adding `+5` preserves Minimum Spanning Trees."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Adding `+C` (`C = 5`) to every edge adds `k * C` to a `k`-edge path, penalizing paths with more edges."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "Let path `P_1` have `k_1` edges and path `P_2` have `k_2` edges. Under `w'(e) = w(e) + C`, `w'(P_1) = w(P_1) + k_1 C` and `w'(P_2) = w(P_2) + k_2 C`. Whenever `k_1 != k_2`, the shift `+C` adds a *different* bias `k_1 C != k_2 C` to the two paths, biasing toward fewer-hop paths. (By contrast, Johnson's algorithm reweights `w^(u, v) = w(u, v) + h(u) - h(v)` via telescoping potentials so *every* `s-t` path shifts by the exact same constant `h(s) - h(t)` independent of hop count `k`).",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Dijkstra runs fine on `w' >= 0`, it just computes the shortest path with respect to the *wrong* objective `w(P) + k*C`.",
        "B": "Multiplying negative weights by `+5` keeps them negative (`-5 * 5 = -25`).",
        "C": "In MST, every spanning tree has the same edge count `k = V - 1`; in Shortest Paths, competing `s-t` paths have *different* edge counts `k_1 != k_2`."
      },
