      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "answer_explanation": "Shortest vs Longest Simple Path is the canonical illustration of Optimal Substructure and subproblem independence: in `G` with cycles, a longest simple path from `u` to `v` through intermediate vertex `w` cannot be decomposed into 'longest simple path `u -> w` + longest simple path `w -> v`' because those two paths may share vertices, violating the simple path requirement (subproblems are not independent). Shortest simple path exhibits independent optimal subproblems.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Shortest paths never benefit from repeating vertices when cycle weights are non-negative, so subproblems do not conflict over used vertices.",
        "B": "If Longest Simple Path had polynomial independent optimal substructure on general graphs, it would be solvable in polynomial time, but Longest Simple Path is NP-hard.",
        "D": "Shortest Path is the foundation of Bellman-Ford and Floyd-Warshall DP."
      },
