      "learning_objective_id": "T13_1_LO3",
      "question_category": "code_trace",
      "stem": "Consider the Coin Change (Minimum Coins) problem: given coin denominations `coins = {1, 3, 4}` and target sum `S = 6`, we want the minimum number of coins needed to make `S = 6` (unlimited supply of each coin). What is the DP recurrence for `dp[x]` (minimum coins for sum `x`, with `dp[0] = 0`), and what is `dp[6]` compared to the Greedy choice (`4 + 1 + 1`)?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Coin Change with `{1, 3, 4}` cannot be solved by Dynamic Programming because subproblems `dp[x - c]` do not overlap."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "`dp[x] = dp[x - 1] + dp[x - 3] + dp[x - 4]`; for target `S = 6`, the recurrence evaluates to `dp[6] = 9` minimum coins."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "`dp[6] = 3` (`4 + 1 + 1`), matching the Greedy algorithm."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "`dp[x] = 1 + min_{c <= x} dp[x - c]`; for `S = 6`, `dp[0..6] = [0, 1, 2, 1, 1, 2, 2]`, giving `dp[6] = 2` (`3 + 3`) vs Greedy's `3`."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "Trace `dp[0..6]` for `coins = {1, 3, 4}`:\n- `dp[0] = 0`\n- `dp[1] = 1 + dp[0] = 1`\n- `dp[2] = 1 + dp[1] = 2`\n- `dp[3] = 1 + min(dp[2], dp[0]) = 1`\n- `dp[4] = 1 + min(dp[3], dp[1], dp[0]) = 1`\n- `dp[5] = 1 + min(dp[4], dp[2], dp[1]) = 2` (`4+1` or `3+1+1`)\n- `dp[6] = 1 + min(dp[5]=2, dp[3]=1, dp[2]=2) = 1 + 1 = 2` (`3 + 3`).",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "`dp[6]` queries `dp[5], dp[3], dp[2]`, which all share smaller states `dp[0..4]`.",
        "B": "`dp[x-1] + dp[x-3] + dp[x-4]` counts the number of ordered *compositions* summing to `x`, not the minimum number of coins.",
        "C": "`3 + 3 = 6` uses only `2` coins, beating Greedy's `4 + 1 + 1` (`3` coins)."
      },
