      "concept_id": "C13",
      "topic_id": "T13_2",
      "learning_objective_id": "T13_2_LO1",
      "question_category": "concept_invariant",
      "stem": "Let `c[i][j]` denote the length of an LCS of prefixes `X[1..i]` and `Y[1..j]` (`1`-indexed, for `0 <= i <= m` and `0 <= j <= n`). What is the standard Dynamic Programming recurrence for `c[i][j]`?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Base case: `c[i][0] = 0` and `c[0][j] = 0`; for `i, j >= 1`: if `X[i] == Y[j]`, `c[i][j] = max(c[i - 1][j], c[i][j - 1]) + 1`; otherwise `c[i][j] = c[i - 1][j - 1]`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "If `X[i] == Y[j]`, `c[i][j] = c[i - 1][j - 1] + 1`; otherwise `c[i][j] = 0`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Base case: `c[i][0] = 0` and `c[0][j] = 0`; for `i, j >= 1`: if `X[i] == Y[j]`, `c[i][j] = c[i - 1][j - 1] + 1`; otherwise `c[i][j] = max(c[i - 1][j], c[i][j - 1])`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Base case: `c[i][0] = 1` and `c[0][j] = 1`; `c[i][j] = c[i - 1][j] + c[i][j - 1]`."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "answer_explanation": "When `X[i] == Y[j]`, appending this matching character to any LCS of `X[1..i-1]` and `Y[1..j-1]` gives `c[i-1][j-1] + 1`. When `X[i] != Y[j]`, any common subsequence of `X[1..i]` and `Y[1..j]` cannot end with *both* `X[i]` and `Y[j]`, so it is either in `(X[1..i-1], Y[1..j])` or in `(X[1..i], Y[1..j-1])`, giving `max(c[i-1][j], c[i][j-1])`.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Using `max(c[i-1][j], c[i][j-1]) + 1` when `X[i] == Y[j]` would allow a single character in `X[i]` (or `Y[j]`) to be matched twice (`X=\"A\", Y=\"AA\"` would falsely give length `2`.).",
        "B": "Resetting `c[i][j] = 0` when `X[i] != Y[j]` solves *Longest Common Contiguous Substring*, NOT Longest Common Subsequence.",
        "D": "An empty prefix (`i = 0` or `j = 0`) has LCS length `0`, not `1`."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "easy",
