      "concept_id": "C13",
      "topic_id": "T13_2",
      "learning_objective_id": "T13_2_LO3",
      "question_category": "code_trace",
      "stem": "Suppose we have already computed the `(m + 1) x (n + 1)` LCS length table `c[0..m][0..n]` (without storing a separate `b[i][j]` arrow table). How can we reconstruct an LCS string directly from `c`, and what is the time complexity of this reconstruction step?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Reconstruction is impossible without storing a separate `char b[m+1][n+1]` arrow table."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Scan all `m * n` cells of `c` in row-major order and output `X[i - 1]` whenever `c[i][j] > c[i - 1][j - 1]`, taking `O(m * n)` time."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Start at `(i = m, j = n)`: while `i > 0 && j > 0`, if `X[i - 1] == Y[j - 1]`, record `X[i - 1]` and step diagonally; else if `c[i - 1][j] >= c[i][j - 1]`, step up (`--i;`); else step left (`--j;`), then reverse in `O(m + n)` time."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Start at `(i = 1, j = 1)` and greedily jump to the first matching character in `Y`."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "answer_explanation": "At every step of the backward walk from `(m, n)`, either `i` decreases by 1, `j` decreases by 1, or both decrease by 1. Therefore the loop executes at most `m + n` iterations (`O(m + n)` time.) and needs zero extra `b[i][j]` arrow table because `X[i-1] == Y[j-1]` and comparing `c[i-1][j]` vs `c[i][j-1]` tells us the exact transition.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "The arrow table `b[i][j]` is purely pedagogical; `X[i-1] == Y[j-1]` and `c[i-1][j] >= c[i][j-1]` recover the arrow in `O(1)` time.",
        "B": "Printing every cell with `c[i][j] > c[i-1][j-1]` prints characters from *all* branches of the table (`O(mn)` characters), not a single valid path of length `<= min(m, n)`.",
        "D": "Greedy forward matching fails on `X = \"AB\", Y = \"BA\"` (matching `'A'` at `Y[2]` skips `'B'`, which is fine here, or on `X = \"ABC\", Y = \"BCAB\"` where greedy `'A'` at `Y[3]` gets `\"AB\"` of length 2 instead of `\"BC\"` or on `X = \"AXBYC\", Y = \"XYABC\"` where matching `'A'` at `Y[3]` gets `\"ABC\"` vs `\"XYC\"`)."
      },
