      "learning_objective_id": "T13_2_LO3",
      "question_category": "code_trace",
      "stem": "While reconstructing an LCS for `X = \"BD\"` and `Y = \"DB\"` (where `c[2][2] = 1` and both `\"B\"` and `\"D\"` are valid LCSs of length 1), at cell `(i = 2, j = 2)` we have `X[1] = 'D' != Y[1] = 'B'` and a tie `c[1][2] == c[2][1] == 1`. What happens depending on whether our tie-breaking rule chooses `c[i - 1][j] >= c[i][j - 1]` (move UP on tie) vs `c[i - 1][j] > c[i][j - 1]` (move LEFT on tie)?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Ties `c[i - 1][j] == c[i][j - 1]` never occur in an LCS table."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Moving LEFT at a tie produces an invalid string that is not a subsequence of `X`, so tie-breaking must always move UP."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Both tie-breaking rules always return the exact same string."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Moving UP discards `X[1] = 'D'` and finds LCS `\"B\"`, whereas moving LEFT discards `Y[1] = 'B'` and finds LCS `\"D\"`—showing that ties `c[i - 1][j] == c[i][j - 1]` when `X[i-1] != Y[j-1]` branch between distinct optimal LCS strings."
        }
      ],
