      "answer_explanation": "When `X[i-1] != Y[j-1]` and `c[i-1][j] == c[i][j-1]`, both subproblems `(X[1..i-1], Y[1..j])` and `(X[1..i], Y[1..j-1])` achieve the same maximum LCS length `c[i][j]`. Choosing UP explores one optimal branch (`\"B\"`), while choosing LEFT explores the other (`\"D\"`).",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Even `c[0][0] == 0` or any mismatched pair after equal lengths creates ties.",
        "B": "Both `c[i-1][j]` and `c[i][j-1]` equal `c[i][j] = 1`, so either direction preserves optimal length and validity.",
        "C": "On `X = \"BD\", Y = \"DB\"`, UP yields `\"B\"` and LEFT yields `\"D\"`."
      },
