      "learning_objective_id": "T13_2_LO3",
      "question_category": "code_trace",
      "stem": "Why can we NOT immediately reconstruct the actual LCS string in `O(m + n)` time if we only kept the 2-row rolling array `prev[0..n]` and `curr[0..n]` (discarding rows `0..m-2`), and what famous algorithm combines Divide-and-Conquer with 2-row LCS to reconstruct the full LCS string in `O(m * n)` time and `O(m + n)` linear space?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "We can reconstruct the full LCS string directly from the final row `curr[0..n]` in `O(n)` time by printing `Y[j - 1]` at every column `j` where `curr[j] > curr[j - 1]`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Backward path reconstruction needs earlier rows `1..m-1` overwritten by the 2-row rolling array; Hirschberg's Algorithm solves this in `O(m * n)` time and `O(m + n)` space by running 2-row forward/backward LCS to split at `k^* = argmax_k (L_1[k] + L_2[n-k])` and recursing."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Hirschberg's algorithm takes `O(m * n * log m)` time because of recursion depth `log m`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Reconstructing the LCS string in `O(m + n)` space is mathematically impossible in polynomial time."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "B",
      "answer_explanation": "Notice why simply checking `curr[j] > curr[j - 1]` on the final row `m` fails: in `X = \"AB\", Y = \"BA\"`, row 2 is `curr = [0, 1, 1]`, where `curr[j]` increases only at `j = 1` (`Y[0] = 'B'`), working for length 1, but on `X = \"ABC\", Y = \"ACB\"`, row 3 is `[0, 1, 2, 2]`, increasing at `Y[0]='A'` and `Y[1]='C'` (`\"AC\"`), while on `X = \"BC\", Y = \"CB\"` or `X = \"AB\", Y = \"CAB\"` (`curr = [0, 0, 1, 2]`), or `X = \"ABC\", Y = \"CAB\"` (`curr = [0, 1, 1, 2]`, increases at `Y[0]='C'` and `Y[2]='B'`, outputting `\"CB\"` which is NOT a subsequence of `X = \"ABC\"`.). Hirschberg's D&C + 2-row DP finds the exact midpoint split in geometric series time `mn(1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + ...) = 2mn = O(mn)` and `O(m + n)` space.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Counterexample: for `X = \"ABC\"` and `Y = \"CAB\"`, the final row `c[3][0..3]` is `[0, 1, 1, 2]`. The columns where `c[3][j] > c[3][j-1]` are `j = 1 ('C')` and `j = 3 ('B')`, which gives `\"CB\"`—NOT a subsequence of `X = \"ABC\"`. (Because `c[3][1] = 1` came from `'C'`, while `c[3][3] = 2` came from `\"AB\"`.).",
        "C": "Because the area of the two recursive subproblems `(m/2) * k + (m/2) * (n - k) = m * n / 2` halves at every level, the geometric sum `1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + ... < 2` gives `O(m * n)` time, NOT `O(m * n log m)`.",
        "D": "Hirschberg's (1975) algorithm achieves `O(m * n)` time and `O(m + n)` space."
      },
