      "question_category": "concept_invariant",
      "stem": "Why do practical routing systems almost always pair a Constructive Heuristic (like Nearest Neighbor or Multi-Fragment Greedy) with a subsequent Local Search phase (like 2-Opt)?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "A Constructive Heuristic outputs an incomplete partial tour visiting fewer than `n` cities, and Local Search is required to insert the missing cities."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "A Constructive Heuristic builds a starting tour in `O(n^2)` time, and Local Search (2-Opt) iteratively untangles crossings to reduce tour cost."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Because Local Search can run without any initial tour."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Pairing Nearest Neighbor with 2-Opt guarantees finding the exact global optimum on every graph in `O(n^2)` time."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "B",
      "answer_explanation": "Constructive heuristics and perturbative local search are complementary: Nearest Neighbor rapidly constructs a reasonable initial tour (`O(n^2)`), and 2-Opt then examines pairs of non-adjacent tour edges to eliminate crossings and greedy end-of-tour spikes, typically reducing tour excess over optimal from `~20-25%` down to `~2-5%`.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Constructive heuristics always output a complete valid Hamiltonian tour of all `n` cities.",
        "C": "Local search requires a complete initial feasible solution `s_0` to perturb.",
        "D": "2-Opt converges to a *local* optimum (2-optimal tour), which is much better than raw NN but not guaranteed to be the global optimum."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "hard",
