      "concept_id": "C14",
      "topic_id": "T14_3",
      "learning_objective_id": "T14_3_LO2",
      "question_category": "code_trace",
      "stem": "In a symmetric undirected TSP instance (`d(u, v) == d(v, u)`), why can we evaluate the exact change in total tour cost `Delta` of a candidate 2-Opt move (reversing `T[i+1 .. j]`) in `O(1)` constant time instead of recomputing the sum of all `n` tour edges in `O(n)` time?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Evaluating a 2-Opt move always requires `O(n)` time to reverse the array *before* checking if the cost improved."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Evaluating `Delta` for a single 2-Opt candidate move requires summing all `n` edges of the reversed tour from scratch in `Theta(n)` time."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "`O(1)` evaluation also works unmodified on *asymmetric* directed TSP where `d(u, v) != d(v, u)` without any extra preprocessing."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Reversing `T[i+1.. j]` leaves all `n - 2` other undirected edges unchanged; only 2 edges are removed and 2 added, so `Delta = (d(T[i], T[j]) + d(T[i+1], T[(j+1)%n])) - (d(T[i], T[i+1]) + d(T[j], T[(j+1)%n]))` takes `O(1)` time."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "In symmetric TSP, traversing the internal edges of `T[i+1 .. j]` backward (`t_j -> ... -> t_{i+1}`) has the exact same total weight as traversing them forward (`t_{i+1} -> ... -> t_j`). Thus we only check whether `d(t_i, t_j) + d(t_{i+1}, t_{j+1}) < d(t_i, t_{i+1}) + d(t_j, t_{j+1})` in `O(1)` time, and we only perform the `O(n)` `std::reverse` when an actual improvement (`Delta < 0`) is accepted.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "You never reverse the array just to *test* a candidate pair `(i, j)`; you test `Delta < 0` in `O(1)` first.",
        "B": "The reversed segment `T[i+1 .. j]` can have any length from `2` to `n - 2`.",
        "C": "Subtle insight: in *asymmetric* directed TSP (`d(u, v) != d(v, u)`), reversing `T[i+1 .. j]` flips the direction of every internal edge in that segment, changing their weights too."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "MC_2OPT_O1_DELTA_EVALUATION_SYMMETRIC"
      ],
      "authorship_type": "AI-assisted team-authored",
