      "correct_option_id": "A",
      "answer_explanation": "Proof sketch of `(1) <=> (2) <=> (3)`:\n- `(1) => (2)`: If `G_f` had an augmenting path `p`, augmenting along `p` would strictly increase `|f|`, contradicting that `f` is maximum.\n- `(2) => (3)`: If `G_f` has no `s-t` path, let `S = {v in V : v is reachable from s in G_f}` and `T = V \\ S`. Since `t not in S`, `(S, T)` is a valid `s-t` cut. Because no residual edge crosses from `S` to `T` (`c_f(u, v) == 0` for all `u in S, v in T`), every forward edge `(u, v) in S x T` has `f(u, v) = c(u, v)` and every backward edge `(v, u) in T x S` has `f(v, u) = 0`, proving `|f| = f(S, T) = c(S, T)`.\n- `(3) => (1)`: By weak duality (`|f'| <= c(S, T)` for all flows `f'`), `|f| = c(S, T)` implies `f` is a maximum flow and `(S, T)` is a minimum cut.",
